<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/columbia-river.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/columbia-river-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/columbia-river.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/cities-we-serve/">Cities</a></li><li aria-current="page">Vancouver</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Southwest Washington</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser for Vancouver, WA</h1><p class="lede">Across the Columbia from Portland, Vancouver gets the Columbia Gorge’s cold east winds and ice storms. Its fast-growing subdivisions and older core neighborhoods each bring their own claim issues.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-y" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Clark County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Southwest Washington</dd></div><div><dt>Inspections</dt><dd>On site</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="note local"><b class="h">Local claim risks in Vancouver</b><ul class="chips"><li>Ice storms</li><li>Windstorms</li><li>Falling trees</li><li>Frozen pipes</li><li>Rare tornadoes</li></ul><p class="housing"><b>Housing mix:</b> Older homes near downtown, mid-century ranches, large newer subdivisions and riverfront condos.</p></div>

<h2>Gorge winds and ice</h2>
<p>Vancouver is the seat of Clark County and the fourth-largest city in Washington. Its weather is shaped by the Columbia River Gorge. In winter, cold air from the east can pour through the Gorge and meet Pacific moisture, producing freezing rain and ice storms. The February 2021 ice storm and the January 2024 ice storm both coated trees and power lines across Clark County, bringing down limbs and leaving many homes without heat.</p>
<p>Vancouver also has one of the more notable tornadoes in state history. On April 5, 1972, a tornado tore through parts of the city, damaging a shopping center, a school and many homes. Tornadoes remain rare, but strong spring and autumn storms can still cause wind damage.</p>
<h2>Housing in Clark County</h2>
<p>The Hough and Arnada neighborhoods near downtown have older homes. Neighborhoods across east Vancouver have mid-century ranches. Much of the growth in Camas, Ridgefield, Battle Ground and Salmon Creek is newer subdivisions with open floor plans and composition roofs. The downtown waterfront has newer condominium buildings.</p>
<h2>Common Vancouver claims</h2>
<ul>
<li>Ice-storm tree and limb damage to roofs, gutters and fences.</li>
<li>Frozen pipes during long outages and cold snaps.</li>
<li>Wind-damaged roofing and siding on exposed elevations.</li>
<li>Water losses in newer homes with open, connected floor plans.</li>
</ul>

